referenceSolutions.python:
def pairedDrawersPositiveProofStrip(drawers, k):
    a=drawers
    out=[]
    i=j=0
    while i<len(a[0]) or j<len(a[1]):
        if j==len(a[1]) or (i<len(a[0]) and a[0][i]<=a[1][j]):
            out.append(a[0][i])
            i+=1
        else:
            out.append(a[1][j])
            j+=1
    return [x for x in out if x>0]

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int[] pairedDrawersPositiveProofStrip(int[][] drawers, int k) {
    int[][] a=drawers; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int i=0,j=0;while(i<a[0].length||j<a[1].length){if(j==a[1].length||(i<a[0].length&&a[0][i]<=a[1][j]))out.add(a[0][i++]);else out.add(a[1][j++]);}}
    return out.stream().filter(x->x>0).mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

The input contains exactly two sorted drawers. Produce all their readings in one sorted sequence, preserving duplicates.

Return only the strictly positive values in the derived sequence, preserving their relative order and duplicates.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n); space O(n).

